        "excerpt": "Institute of Petroleum Engineering,  Heriot-Watt University  \n7\n22\nRock and Fluid Properties\n                      \n \nFigure 5  Secondary Porosity in Limestone\n4 SATURATION\nSaturation of any given fluid in a pore space is the ratio of the volume of the fluid \nto the total pore space volume.  For example, a water saturation of 10% means that \n1/10 of the pore space is filled with water.\nWhere porosity is the capacity to hold fluids,  saturation is the percentage or fraction \nof this total capacity that actually holds any particular fluid.  Porosity, hydrocarbon \nsaturation, the thickness of the reservoir rock and the areal extent of the reservoir rock \nall contribute to the total hydrocarbons in place (Figures 6 and 7).  These establish \nthe economic potential of any given reservoir.\n                                           \nOil\nWater\n \nFigure 6  Reservoir rock - saturation with different fluids (oil and water)"
